messages:
customer_jordan: Hey team — our app deployed on Vercel Edge is intermittently getting CORS failures when the browser calls the Redwood edge SDK. In Safari the preflight shows an opaque/204 response and the request fails with `TypeError: Failed to fetch`. Chrome works fine locally and in prod sometimes. Anyone seen this?

elaine_support: Sorry you're hitting this, Jordan. A couple quick questions: which SDK package/version are you using (edge vs node bundle)? Are you sending cookies/credentials or an Authorization header from the browser? Also can you paste the exact client call or fetch snippet?

customer_jordan: We're using the TypeScript SDK edge bundle (v0.8.2) in a Next.js app with `runtime: "edge"` API route that proxies to Redwood hosted API. From the browser we call: 

```js
// client code in browser
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference-js-edge';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RW_KEY });
await client.chat.create({ model: 'gpt-redwood-1', input: 'hi' });
```

No cookies sent, we rely on the public API key in env. Network devtools show: OPTIONS preflight -> 204 with no CORS headers -> browser treats it as opaque in Safari and fails the main POST.

alisa_devex: Safari is more strict about opaque responses on preflight. Also Vercel Edge will return a default 204 for OPTIONS if your edge function doesn't explicitly handle OPTIONS, which results in missing CORS headers. Try adding an explicit OPTIONS handler in your Next.js API route to return the proper Access-Control-Allow-* headers.

nate_eng: Fwiw, we also have a known issue where the edge browser bundle accidentally set credentials mode in some builds (causing credentialed preflight expectations). We're investigating a fix. If the browser request is sent with `credentials: 'include'` it requires `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. Do you see `credentials` set anywhere?

customer_jordan: We didn't set credentials explicitly. I dug into devtools and the Request headers for OPTIONS include `origin` and `access-control-request-headers: authorization,content-type` even though we don't send Authorization. Strange. Headers are varying between Chrome and Safari.

elaine_support: Quick mitigation you can try on the Vercel edge route: intercept OPTIONS and return CORS headers explicitly. Example: 

```js
// nextjs edge api route (simplified)
export default async function handler(req) {
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    return new Response(null, {
      status: 204,
      headers: {
        'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': req.headers.get('origin') || '*',
        'Access-Control-Allow-Methods': 'POST, OPTIONS',
        'Access-Control-Allow-Headers': 'authorization, content-type',
        'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials': 'true'
      }
    });
  }
  // existing proxy logic to Redwood
}
```

Note: if you don't want credentialed requests, set `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: false` (or omit) and make sure client requests don't use credentials.

nate_eng: As a temporary SDK-side workaround, you can force the fetch options to omit credentials when calling the client. Example (not ideal but quick): 

```js
// pass fetch options through client call
await client.chat.create({ model: 'gpt-redwood-1', input: 'hi' }, { fetchOptions: { credentials: 'omit' } });
```

We'll ship a patch in 0.8.3 to ensure the edge/browser bundle doesn't enable credentialed mode by default. ETA: later today.

deploybot: deploy succeeded: main -> production @ 1842102000 :rocket:

alisa_devex: I'll add a short doc snippet about Vercel Edge OPTIONS and common CORS pitfalls (Safari) and link the SDK workaround until the patch is out.

customer_jordan: Adding the explicit OPTIONS handler fixed it for me in Safari. Thanks all :tada: Will test again after the SDK patch.

nate_eng: Great — please re-open thread if you see it after 0.8.3. If it recurs, we'll ask for a minimal public repro repo to debug the edge behavior.
